'''''Oenothera drummondii''''', the '''beach evening-primrose''' or '''Drummond's evening-primrose''', is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Onagraceae]].<ref name="OEDR" >{{cite web |url=https://www.wildflower.org/plants/result.php?id_plant=OEDR |title=''Oenothera drummondii'' Hook. |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=16 November 2022 |website=Plant Database |publisher=Lady Bird Johnson Wildflower Center |access-date=17 November 2023 }}</ref> It is native to Mexico and the southeastern United States, and it has been introduced to many locations around the world.<ref name="POWO_294786-2" >{{cite POWO |id=294786-2 |title=''Oenothera drummondii'' Hook. |access-date=17 November 2023 }}</ref> It is found on coastal dunes and other disturbed sandy areas at elevations below {{cvt|400|m}}.<ref name="FoC_250073358" >{{cite web |url=http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=2&taxon_id=250073358 |title=海滨月见草 hai bin yue jian cao |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2023 |website=Flora of China |publisher=efloras.org |access-date=17 November 2023 }}</ref> 

==Subtaxa==
The following subspecies are accepted:<ref name=" POWO_294786-2" />
*''Oenothera drummondii'' subsp. ''drummondii'' – Texas, Louisiana, Florida, South Carolina, North Carolina, Mexico Northeast, Mexico Gulf, Mexico Southeast, introduced worldwide
*''Oenothera drummondii'' subsp. ''thalassaphila'' {{small|(Brandegee) W.Dietr. & W.L.Wagner}} – southern [[Baja California]]
